  <section>
    <h2>3. Seasonal & Calendar-Based Patterns</h2>
    <p>We test monthly and day-of-week effects using one-way ANOVA on aggregated log returns.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Monthly seasonality:</strong> F = {kpis['f_stat_monthly']:.2f}, p = {kpis['p_value_monthly']:.3f} → <strong>not statistically significant</strong> at conventional levels. Observed differences are likely noise.</li>
      <li><strong>Day-of-week seasonality:</strong> F = {kpis['f_stat_dow']:.2f}, p = {kpis['p_value_dow']:.3f} → <strong>not statistically significant</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That said, the <em>point estimates</em> show January and August as the strongest months on average, while June is the weakest. Analysts should treat any month-trading strategy with caution given the high variance.</p>
